 We Knew 409\nL69@P10:  Starting Points: Thoughts for Managing a Creative Culture 427\nL70@P10:  Acknowledgments 433\nL71@P10:  Index 437\nL72@P10-11: Copyrighted MaterialINTRODUCTION TO THE EXPANDED EDITION\nL73@P11: It’s been almost a decade since Creativity, Inc. was fi rst published.\nL74@P11: During that time, I’ve had the opportunity to talk to thousands of\nL75@P11: readers. Those conversations have always been gratifying, as peo\u0002ple from all walks of life describe how the book has helped them\nL76@P11: try to foster and support creativity in their own workplaces. But\nL77@P11: some readers came away with impressions that I did not intend,\nL78@P11: and I began to wonder if I could have been clearer in some of my\nL79@P11: explanations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nprelims (https://carlotaperez.org/wp-content/downloads/books/btr-en/PrefaceCF.pdf)\nciteturn54view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://carlotaperez.org/wp-content/downloads/books/btr-en/PrefaceCF.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 120\nL0@P0: chapter title ix\nL1@P0: ix\nL2@P0: Carlota Perez has made several highly original contributions to the\nL3@P0: understanding of long-term technological transformations and the way in which\nL4@P0: such changes interact with wider economic, social and political changes. This\nL5@P0: book is perhaps her most original and controversial contribution. Her intense\nL6@P0: interest in these deep processes was aroused in the 1970s when, as a young\nL7@P0: researcher, she was studying the oil industry, then and still today of critical\nL8@P0: importance for her own native country, Venezuela. In trying to explain the\nL9@P0: causes and consequences of the so-called OPEC crisis of 1973, she became\nL10@P0: convinced that the global economy had begun a long-term transition from a\nL11@P0: mass-production economy based on cheap oil to an ‘information economy’\nL12@P0: based on cheap micro-electronics. The arrival of the microprocessor – a\nL13@P0: ‘computer on a chip’ – served as a ‘big-bang’ announcing this probability and\nL14@P0: she was able to develop her theory at this time through a period of postgraduate\nL15@P0: research in California – a state which was already then at the forefront of the\nL16@P0: Information Revolution.\nL17@P0: As a result of this research and her subsequent work with government and\nL18@P0: industry, she was able to publish in 1983 a paper that became an influential\nL19@P0: landmark in this field. It was entitled ‘Structural Change and the Assimilation\nL20@P0: of New Technologies in the Economic and Social System’ and the title ad\u0002equately reflects the content. It became influential for three main reasons. First\nL21@P0: of all, it demonstrated that very big changes in technology entailed not just the\nL22@P0: extraordinarily rapid growth of a few new industries, but also, over a more\nL23@P0: prolonged period, the rejuvenation of many ‘old’ industries, which found ways\nL24@P0: to use the new technology and to make changes in their organization and man\u0002agement, influenced by the new industries. She designated this combination\nL25@P0: of new ways of thinking about the productive system, including its organiza\u0002tion, its techniques and its interdependencies as a change of ‘techno-economic\nL26@P0: paradigm’. This concept of a paradigm change, with each major technological\nL27@P0: revolution, has become very widely accepted, particularly since Alan Greenspan\nL28@P0: began to use the expression in the 1990s to explain the upsurge in the Ameri\u0002can economy at that time.\nL29@P0: The second major contribution which Carlota Perez made in that paper was\nL30@P0: to point out that such a ‘meta-paradigm’ change, affecting the entire economy\nL31@P0: entailed the very widespread use of new inputs. In each technological revolu\u0002tion, whether in earlier times with iron, coal, steel, or oil or with chips today, it\nL32@P0-1: Prefacex Technological Revolutions and Financial Capital\nL33@P1: was possible to make very great economies of scale in the production of these\nL34@P1: inputs, and frequently such a steep decline in price followed that they became\nL35@P1: very attractive for economic as well as for purely technical reasons.\nL36@P1: Finally, she showed up some of the fallacies of what is known to historians\nL37@P1: as ‘technological determinism’ by her insistence that any transformation in\nL38@P1: technology could only take place through an interactive and accompanying\nL39@P1: process of social, political and managerial change. This meant that the change\nL40@P1: of paradigm affected not only management and organization at the level of the\nL41@P1: firm but it affected and was affected by the entire system of social and political\nL42@P1: regulation. This is particularly obvious in such areas as education and training,\nL43@P1: where the strong demand for new skills drives the changes, but it is also appar\u0002ent in the intellectual property regime (trademarks, patents and so on) and the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://andrewchen.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/ColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.pdf)\nciteturn54view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 400; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://andrewchen.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/ColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 8868\nL0@P0: THE\nL1@P0: COLD\nL2@P0: START\nL3@P0: PROBLEM\nL4@P0-2: ColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.indd 1 9/28/21 4:42 PMColdStartProb_9780062969743_AS0928_cc20_Final.indd 2 9/28/21 4:42 PMTHE\nL5@P2: COLD\nL6@P2: START\nL7@P2: PROBLEM\nL8@P2: HOW TO START\nL9@P2: AND SCALE\nL10@P2: NETWORK EFFECTS\nL11@P2: ANDREW CHEN\nL12@P2-3: ColdStartProb_9780